## Behind a CDN of your own

A rule matches the address the platform's edge sees the connection come from.
When a CDN or a proxy of your own sits in front of the app, that is the CDN's
address: every visitor arrives from the CDN's ranges, and the visitor's own
address is in a header the firewall does not read. Two things follow.

- An allowlist of office addresses lets nobody in, because no request arrives
  from the office.
- An allowlist of the CDN's published ranges does work, and is the usual use:
  it keeps out requests sent straight to the platform host. It does not keep
  out the CDN's other customers, whose requests arrive from the same ranges.
  Filtering by visitor is then the CDN's job.

## What an apply waits for

A change is stored at once and enforced by the edge shortly after. An apply
that changes a firewall's rules or default action returns once every app and
website it is attached to enforces the new revision, and one that sets, changes
or removes `firewall` on an app or a bucket returns once the edge enforces the
result. It waits five minutes at most and fails with the edge's own reason if
the edge refuses the change.

An app or a bucket created with `firewall` set is created with the firewall
attached, in the same request, so it is never served without it. Adding
`firewall` to one that already exists leaves it open until the edge has taken
the firewall, a few seconds.

A firewall that is still attached is not deleted: the API names what it is
attached to. Removing `firewall` from the app or the bucket in the same
configuration is enough when it refers to the firewall by `fpcloud_firewall.<name>.id`,
since the detach is then applied first.

## A change that denies the machine running the apply

A firewall filters requests to the apps and websites it is attached to, and
never the API this provider talks to. A change or an attach that denies the
address the apply runs from is therefore made like any other, and the apply
shows a warning saying so. From a CI runner, which is rarely in an allowlist,
that warning is expected. Nothing has to be set to allow it, and the firewall
can always be changed again from the same machine.

## Edits made outside the configuration

The rules in the configuration are the whole list. An apply writes them over
whatever the firewall holds, including a rule somebody added with the CLI or
in the console since the last apply: the plan shows that rule being removed.

An apply only writes over what its plan showed. Every apply writes the whole
firewall in one request, on condition that it is still at the `revision` the
plan read. When somebody changes it between the plan and the apply, the apply
fails with "Firewall changed since the plan" and nothing is written; a new plan
shows their change, and applying that plan is what replaces it.

## Rules are keyed by priority

`rules` is a map from a rule's priority to the rule. The key is the rule's
identifier and its place in the evaluation order, so a plan names each rule by
it:

- adding a rule between two others, such as `150` between `100` and `200`,
  plans that one rule being added and touches no other;
- changing a rule plans a change under its key alone;
- moving a rule to another priority plans the old key removed and the new one
  added, and both happen in the same write, so the rule is never absent.

A key is written as a plain number from 1 to 65535, with no sign, space or
leading zero: `0100` and `+100` are refused, so one priority has one spelling.
Leave gaps between priorities so a rule can be added between two others later.

## Rule order and spelling

Rules may be written in any order: the key decides the order they are
evaluated in, and reordering them in the configuration plans no change. The API
keeps every source as a masked CIDR, so `10.0.0.5` is stored as `10.0.0.5/32`
and `10.1.2.3/8` as `10.0.0.0/8`. Either spelling in the configuration is the
same source and plans no change. An imported firewall is read the way the API
spells it, and plans no change against a configuration that holds the same
rules in any order.

## Upgrading from a list of rules

Earlier versions of this provider took `rules` as a list, with `priority`
inside each rule. Rewrite each rule under its priority and drop the
`priority` attribute:

```terraform
# Before
rules = [
  { priority = 100, action = "allow", match = { sources = ["198.51.100.0/24"] } },
  { priority = 200, action = "deny", match = { sources = ["192.0.2.0/24"] } },
]

# After
rules = {
  100 = { action = "allow", match = { sources = ["198.51.100.0/24"] } }
  200 = { action = "deny", match = { sources = ["192.0.2.0/24"] } }
}
```

State written by an earlier version is converted on the first plan, with no
request to the API beyond the usual refresh, and the rewritten configuration
plans no change against it.

